Both bodies taken are MIT licensed. Their full license texts are reproduced in
LICENSES-THIRD-PARTY.md — kept out of LICENSE so that GitHub's license detector
sees a single unambiguous MIT and labels the repository correctly.

What was changed: between them the upstreams ship an installer, a CLI, a
statusline, slash commands, a mode-tracker state file, intensity levels, evals
and a benchmark suite. The persona prose is the only part that changes model
behavior, so only that was kept — one baked-in intensity, with the
self-referential mode-switching prose ("stop adhd mode", intensity levels,
statusline tags) dropped. Each upstream is still named where its rules begin, so
a reader of the injected text can tell which behaviour came from where. i-have-adhd is folded into the second half of the essence-first axis
rather than shipping as its own body, because the two answer the same question
from opposite ends: one names the idea, the other names the move it implies.
One deliberate inversion: upstream i-have-adhd puts the next action on the first
line, and here the altitude line owns the opening with the action directly
beneath it. This extraction pattern is taken from a private agent-runtime
codebase, which did the same reduction in order to inject personas into a Claude
Agent SDK worker. The measurements behind this project's headline numbers come
from that system; the report is republished, with the operator's identifiers
removed, in docs/BENCHMARK.md.

A third source, ponytail (https://github.com/DietrichGebert/ponytail, MIT,
Copyright (c) 2026 Dietrich Gebert), supplied a "write less" axis in earlier
versions. It was removed when the axes were re-cut around how an answer is
shaped rather than how code is built; no ponytail-derived text remains, so it is
no longer carried in LICENSES-THIRD-PARTY.md. docs/BENCHMARK.md still reports it
as a benchmarked arm, which is a record of what was measured, not shipped code.

